+We study 2048 under \emph{possible play}: the spawn sequence is part of
+an existential plan, rather than an adversary or a random outcome that
+the player must control. For standard $4\times4$ 2048, we give an explicit
+legal trajectory to $131072$ in exactly $32{,}781$ moves and prove that
+no legal trajectory from any ordinary two-tile opening can be shorter.
+Both the complete witness and the universal lower bound are checked by
+Lean~4's kernel. When both starting tiles are required to be 2s, the
+exact minimum is $32{,}782$; a single preparatory move converts a suitable
+two-2s opening into the certificate's two-4s opening. These are
+existential optima, not guarantees against every random spawn sequence.
+
+The lower-bound argument uses mass above a doubling threshold, directly
+under the slide-and-spawn rules. The reachability theorem has no axiom
+dependencies; the exact-optimum theorem uses only Lean's standard
+\texttt{propext} and \texttt{Quot.sound} axioms. Plaintext witnesses and
+two independent executable replayers accompany the formal development.
+
+Separately, we derive a general minimum-move bound, a primed-board
+classification for fastest top-tile games, the score ceiling
+$\Phi_c-4c$ for a $c$-cell board with
+$\Phi_c=\sum_{k=2}^{c+1}(k-1)2^k$, and the game-length bound
+$2^{c+1}-4-c$. On $4\times4$ these ceilings are $3{,}932{,}100$ points
+and $131{,}052$ moves. We characterise the last move of full-chain games
+and the eight corner-snake symmetries. Enumeration checks the small
+boards, including the $2\times2\times2$ cube, and reproduces the
+published $3\times3$ state count. Our $4\times4$ full-chain witness
+uses $65{,}533$ moves; a score-oriented witness reaches $3{,}925{,}224$
+points, leaving attainment of the score ceiling conjectural. We also
+analyse re-randomising undo and honest-play agents. The Lean claim is
+limited to fixed-$4\times4$ reachability, minimum move counts, and their
+supporting lemmas; the remaining results are not thereby formally verified.
 \end{abstract}
